{
  "image_name": "5bd7dc572adbaa4dadfe2ed188eb5ebb.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes ways to use photoexcited “hot carriers” generated in a solar absorber before they lose energy. Overall, it presents a concept of harvesting or redirecting this short-lived high-energy state into different useful functions, including thermoelectrics, catalysis, phase-change behavior, and other functional materials.\n\nThe lower-left panel illustrates a hot-carrier electrochemical device: light enters a solar absorber connected to a hot electrode and a cold electrode, while an external circuit carries electrons. Inside, a redox cycle between reductant and oxidant appears to couple the two sides, implying charge extraction driven by a temperature or energy difference.\n\nThe upper-right panel shows a hot-carrier photovoltaic idea. A solar absorber feeds electrons into an n-type side and holes into a p-type side, with current flowing through an external path to power a load, while a heat sink below helps maintain the required thermal gradient.\n\nThe lower-right panel focuses on catalysis at a surface. Reactants interact with catalysts where localized hot spots and hot carriers appear to lower barriers or provide energy, producing products and heat. Together, the panels emphasize controlling hot-carrier relaxation to convert sunlight into electricity, chemical transformation, and thermal effects.",
